## 4. General state machine and adapter semantics

**ARCHITECT LOCK / proposed refinements.** Store offer state, contract state, each leg's state and each money-moving operation separately. A single COMPLETE flag cannot describe all of them. A funded trade cannot become CANCELLED, EXPIRED or FAILED merely through a UI timeout.

```mermaid
stateDiagram-v2
    [*] --> OFFERED
    OFFERED --> RESERVED: authenticated take reservation
    OFFERED --> CANCELLED: valid cancellation before commitment
    OFFERED --> EXPIRED: offer expiry
    RESERVED --> CONTRACTED: both authenticate exact contract
    RESERVED --> EXPIRED: no funded obligations
    CONTRACTED --> FUNDING: recovery paths verified
    FUNDING --> FUNDED: required locks independently proven
    FUNDED --> PAYMENT_IN_PROGRESS
    PAYMENT_IN_PROGRESS --> PAYMENT_OBSERVED
    PAYMENT_OBSERVED --> FINALIZING
    FINALIZING --> SETTLED: all required effects finalized
    FUNDING --> REFUNDABLE: adapter proves eligibility
    FUNDED --> REFUNDABLE: adapter proves eligibility
    REFUNDABLE --> REFUNDED: all required returns proven
    PAYMENT_IN_PROGRESS --> DISPUTED
    FINALIZING --> DISPUTED: conflict or lost finality
    DISPUTED --> MEDIATION
    MEDIATION --> ARBITRATION: only if approved policy exists
    DISPUTED --> FINALIZING: reconciled facts
    DISPUTED --> REFUNDABLE: valid recovery path
```

The diagram is a high-level projection, not permission for every adapter to use every edge. Adapter lifecycle PREPARE, FUNDED, PAYMENT_OBSERVED, FINALIZING, SETTLED, REFUNDABLE, REFUNDED, DISPUTED and FAILED_TYPED_REASON is derived from validated facts. Funding order is protocol-specific. PARTIAL/PENDING evidence and per-leg conflicts remain visible. Typed transient errors retain the last proven state; terminal failure requires proof that no live lock, payment or refund obligation was lost. Post-settlement reorg records a new settlement-uncertainty revision and freezes new effects; it does not delete the prior receipt or create a new TradeID.

## 5. Decentralized discovery, cancellation and single-fill limits

**FORGE ENGINEERING DECISION / proposed design.** Independently operated peers gossip compact offer inventories; receivers request selected objects. Local indexes validate version/context/size/expiry before expensive signature work, then verify maker trade authentication. No relay result is authoritative. Manual peer provisioning and cached peers remain possible; optional bootstrap lists supply reachability only, never admission authority. Inventory, body, signature-work and per-peer quotas have separate budgets, bounded TTLs and bounded eviction. A trade identity is cheap to replace: per-identity limits alone do not resist Sybils. Combine connection/traffic budgets, fair scheduling, duplicate suppression and measured admission costs; paid deposits/resource fees require Architect economics.

Maker disconnect makes an offer unavailable for safe new negotiation, not an automatic proof of cancellation. Signed cancellations and terminal tombstones outlive the offer's replay horizon; expiry stays in the signed body. Reconnection retrieves bounded deltas and tombstones. Expired objects cannot obtain a fresh lifetime from relay receipt time. An amended offer is a new signed offer with explicit supersession, not mutation of accepted terms. Local persistence serializes cancel/take and concurrent takers; a durable reservation is committed before an acceptance reply. Retrying the same take returns the same reservation. A funded obligation cannot be cancelled by replaying a public cancellation.

**APPROVED DIRECTION / integration guarantee still to qualify.** Honest-maker local reservations prevent accidental double takes but cannot stop a malicious maker signing two acceptances across partitions or restored journals. Gossip, signatures, reputation and deterministic IDs do not establish global exclusion. The recommended settlement proposal binds each single-fill offer to one canonical consumable reservation/funding anchor and exactly one taker/contract; a taker verifies its final consumption before risking an irreversible leg. Same anchor double spends are resolved by the relevant ledger, not by a matching service. The new MUT OfferConsumption/conditional-lock primitive is approved for D1 candidate engineering; exact final semantics await the consolidated final lock. Its scope must be limited to value-safety, not an on-chain order book. Until a reviewed construction supplies that guarantee, the single-fill release criterion and real-value offer taking remain blocked. Claims of globally unique successful fills must be tested with equivocating makers, not only a mutex.

**Decision-lock correction:** minimal canonical one-time value consumption is approved for candidate implementation. It must be consumed atomically with first canonical funding; reservations alone still cannot prevent equivocation. The isolated candidate keys consumption by full context and OfferID, excluding changing taker/contract/generation fields. It retains tombstones after claim/refund. V2 verifies a hybrid-signed, deterministically identified economic offer and maker consent to the exact operation before consumption; current wallet approvals remain additionally required. Unrelated wallets or trade identities cannot squat a known OfferID. Its global proof depends on one canonically finalized ledger; public discovery, persistent canonical storage and final provider/domain integration remain to qualify.

## 6. Private messaging, privacy transport and threat model

**ARCHITECT LOCK.** Public offers disclose only discovery-critical facts. Payment account details, addresses where not publicly required, chat, negotiated contract, recovery secrets and disputes are private. No wallet spending key enters a relay. Trade identity and session keys are independent of every monetary/system identity.

**PROPOSED engineering path:** adapt the already approved Build 8 X448 + ML-KEM-1024, HKDF-SHA512, ChaCha20-Poly1305 transport profile with trade-specific endpoint identities, roles and transcript domains. Preserve required hybrid authentication; do not invent an Ed448/FN-DSA adaptor protocol. `spec/HYBRID-TRANSPORT-SESSION-V2.md` is component evidence, not ready-made trade onboarding or anonymity. Pin the maker to its verified offer descriptor, the taker to the accepted reservation, and both to the dual-authenticated contract. A peer-provided key alone is not authority. Negotiation transcript and final key confirmation bind OfferID, TradeID, full context, identities, versions and final contract hash. A contract change forces fresh authentication; no unsigned UI strings determine settlement.

Messages carry sender role, monotonic sequence, typed purpose, bounded length, expiry and causal reference. Reject wrong-session/trade/network, downgrade, truncation and duplicate side effects. Bounded reorder windows and acknowledgements reconcile delivery; an ACK proves delivery only. Fresh transport handshakes on restart avoid nonce reuse. Durable message IDs are distinct from per-session AEAD sequences. Store-forward envelopes contain ciphertext for an already established authenticated session or reviewed recipient encryption scheme; no first-contact offline encryption construction is silently invented. Persist nonce allocation before ciphertext release, or abandon that traffic key and start a fresh session. Mailbox quotas and TTL bound compliant local storage; encryption and retrieval authorization limit disclosure. A malicious relay can retain ciphertext and metadata beyond TTL. Crash/replay tests must cover these obligations before enabling offline messaging.

Tor onion-service transport is the recommended first privacy carrier; direct encrypted mode is explicit informed opt-in with conspicuous weaker-privacy status. No automatic clear-net fallback after Tor failure. Tor is a mature privacy transport, not a promise against global correlation or compromised endpoints. Other anonymity carriers require evidence; no custom anonymity cryptography. [Tor v3 specification](https://spec.torproject.org/rend-spec/index.html). Bisq's peer network, Tor use and private trade communication are engineering references only. [Bisq introduction](https://bisq.wiki/Introduction).

| Actor | Learns / can do | Required protection / residual limit |
|---|---|---|
| Maker / taker | Public offers, own private contract and necessary counterparty payment data; can lie, vanish, equivocate or publish chats | Exact contract and chain validation, safe recovery; counterparties can retain disclosed data |
| Offer relay / network peer | Public offer content, timing, inventory requests and route metadata; can censor/reorder/spam | End-to-end signatures, bounded queues, multiple routes; no guaranteed global completeness |
| Full node / Mutiny observer | Canonical transactions, ownership changes, amounts and public Treasury destination | Keep chats/payment data off-chain; no claim that public ledger transfers are anonymous |
| Bitcoin observer | On-chain scripts, amounts, graph, hash/preimage linkage where used | Minimize gratuitous identifiers; conditional swaps can be correlated across chains |
| Mediator / arbitrator | Only deliberately disclosed evidence; can misadvise or leak it | No private keys or implicit payout authority; minimize evidence and authenticate policy |
| Network observer / malicious relay | Timing, sizes and endpoints visible on direct/RF links; can delay or partition | Onion routing where available, padding only after measurement; global traffic analysis remains a risk |
| Compromised UI / coordinator | Can display false terms, request malicious signatures, leak local data | Independent signer reconstructs exact effects from authenticated contract/state; compromise still harms availability/privacy |

Fiat inherently reveals selected account information and can expose civil identity. LoRa exposes local radio presence and traffic patterns; encryption is not anonymity. Public-safe exports must use a whitelist and cannot simply hash low-entropy bank data and call it private. No trade reputation feeds Continuity reputation, voting, bonds or TPM authority.

