## 8. Bitcoin verification, SPV and external signer integration

**ARCHITECT LOCK:** never accept a peer's payment assertion. No miner, relay or ordinary wallet client becomes a Bitcoin oracle. PSBT/watch-only/external hardware signing and separate broadcast remain supported architecture.

No approved Bitcoin header/SPV implementation was identified in the inspected current Build 8 source. Historical entitlement evidence cannot validate current payments. Locate any separately approved source and verify provenance before reuse; otherwise implement and qualify the subsystem behind this adapter. Full-node validation with an authenticated local connection is the preferred reference for qualification. A light-client mode must state its weaker assumptions: headers/PoW/difficulty, chain selection, merkle inclusion and trusted checkpoint/bootstrap policy do not alone prove all Bitcoin consensus rules or global absence of a conflict. Do not call merkle inclusion proof full transaction validity.

The adapter parses bounded canonical Bitcoin serialization, computes exact TXID (and witness identity when relevant), verifies all intended outputs/amounts, authenticated prevout values/ownership/scripts, network, locktime/sequence, fee and approved change. Chain observations bind block hash/height, best-chain lineage, depth, observation revision and conflict/replacement status. Reorgs invalidate affected finality and require exact transaction/input reconciliation. Mempool absent, temporarily unreachable and definitely conflicted are different typed facts. Signed transaction bytes persist before broadcast; lost RPC response triggers exact TXID/input lookup, not a new signature.

PSBT is a transport for transaction/signing metadata, not authority. Reconstruct effects independently, reject substituted UTXOs, unexpected derivation requests, unsafe sighash modes and unknown/unapproved outputs. Do not trust PSBT change labels. Hardware/external signers receive only explicit approved intents and unsigned transactions; returned signed bytes are independently rechecked before a separately authorized broadcast. [BIP174](https://github.com/bitcoin/bips/blob/master/bip-0174.mediawiki).

Replacement is a new authorized artifact linked to the same operation/trade, never an ambiguous retry. Fee policy must account for output conservation, exact consideration, permitted change reduction, input additions, descendant dependencies, old/new TXIDs, pinning and conflicting confirmation. Replacement authority is locked: explicitly authorized, linked to the same operation with invariant economic effects. Technical numeric confirmation/reorg policy may be qualified at the Bitcoin adapter/test-value gate. No generic arbitrary-sign API is exposed.

